Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Analysis", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"f6817e60-8abd-4e00-882c-cc2b92ba9a3f"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Abstract written by Pavel Hajek on 26 October 2026.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->142002610,ExpressionUUID->"e58679f4-e45f-4ae7-a92b-733d6bde380e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The main building block of InfraAnalysis is ",
 StyleBox["InfraCalculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which develops integration and differentiation of functions on the \
vertices of directed acyclic graphs. Integration takes several forms of \
aggregation along the natural direction from sources to sinks, with \
corresponding inverse operations chosen to satisfy a ",
 StyleBox["fundamental theorem of calculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraAntiderivative"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraAntiderivative", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "86f6f818-5810-4427-ab74-61d21e2d6fbf"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraDerivative"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraDerivative", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7e3fc912-948f-4dd6-8bcf-4190dece7222"],
 " are inverse operations for the same integration method. Note that the \
derivative for some forms of integration may be nonlocal."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.00201625320693*^9, 4.0020162609708757`*^9}, {
   4.002016291817523*^9, 4.002016436924316*^9}, {4.002028848686154*^9, 
   4.00202887960938*^9}, 4.002029266384622*^9, {4.002029634054193*^9, 
   4.002029635220296*^9}},
 CellID->1869488933,ExpressionUUID->"f64b6311-daac-4d50-8b41-830b44319861"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Organizing a directed graph into ",
 StyleBox["transversal and radial components",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " according to shortest-path distance from the sources gives a fibered graph \
over a path graph. We can consider its blow-ups and contractions in the \
radial direction and study the ",
 StyleBox["renormalizability of InfraCalculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " by examining their compatibility with integration and differentiation."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.002028893426065*^9, 4.002029079915049*^9}, 
   4.002029280537469*^9},
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"41091be8-c2d9-4abb-8c4b-e0aa896f52e0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Moreover, we have several methods of ",
 StyleBox["coordinatization",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", most notably shortest-path projection onto a collection of orthogonal \
paths, as in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["OrthogonalCoordinates"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/OrthogonalCoordinates", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c0fb8927-d2df-4fa3-babb-1db73b37e28f"],
 ". This gives graphs fibered over a coordinate grid and can be used to \
define ",
 StyleBox["multivariate calculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and study the validity of ",
 StyleBox["Stokes' and Green's theorems",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". We also consider various versions of ",
 StyleBox["differential forms",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and study their relations to cochains on the Rips complex. ",
 StyleBox["Tangent and cotangent graphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", together with Levi-Civita connections and covariant derivatives, are also \
parts of InfraAnalysis."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.002029095650951*^9, 4.002029231323258*^9}, 
   4.002029299799583*^9},
 CellID->705599422,ExpressionUUID->"050ea268-a265-45ff-9e8a-7b8d3efb9c3d"]
}, Open  ]],

